WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.493
Run your own comparison.

2
00:00:01.773 --> 00:00:06.872
Here is a design you can run in an afternoon, and that the lab and the project formalize.

3
00:00:07.152 --> 00:00:09.840
Pick five to ten tasks before you run anything.

4
00:00:10.120 --> 00:00:17.715
Use real, recent work from your repo with a known good outcome: closed bugs with a fixing commit, small features with tests.

5
00:00:17.995 --> 00:00:18.955
Mix types.

6
00:00:19.235 --> 00:00:21.624
Write each as the prompt you’d actually give.

7
00:00:21.904 --> 00:00:23.867
Fix everything except the variable.

8
00:00:24.147 --> 00:00:27.368
Same tasks, same prompt text, same starting commit.

9
00:00:27.648 --> 00:00:31.488
Change one thing: the model, the effort, the tool, or the repo setup.

10
00:00:31.768 --> 00:00:33.091
Isolate each run.

11
00:00:33.371 --> 00:00:38.811
A fresh session in a fresh worktree or clone, so no run sees another’s context or edits.

12
00:00:39.091 --> 00:00:39.901
Repeat.

13
00:00:40.181 --> 00:00:42.272
At least three runs per task per setup.

14
00:00:42.552 --> 00:00:47.715
Agent runs vary, and lesson 2 showed how wide the uncertainty is on small samples.

15
00:00:47.995 --> 00:00:49.829
Record outcomes, not feelings.

16
00:00:50.109 --> 00:00:57.000
For each run: did it succeed by your check (tests, plus your review), how much rework you needed, wall time, and cost.

17
00:00:57.280 --> 00:01:01.845
Decide on cost per successful task and rework, not on the best single run.

18
00:01:02.125 --> 00:01:05.027
The exercise below computes the summary you’d look at.

19
00:01:05.307 --> 00:01:10.981
Keep the conclusion modest: with ten tasks you can detect a large difference, not a small one.
